rts one country's convention, or reads the held-out country's own data). The slice is NEVER counted directly — it is the residual of the four bases already in the file, `age15 = base(11+) − band(11-14) − base(16-74) − band(75+)`, so it cannot drift from them. 🟢 **Independently checked for the UK against `QS103UK` single years: 774,892 − 11,571.87 scaled communal = 763,320.13 vs residual 763,320.32 — AGREE TO 0.19 PERSONS.** Result: `unknown` **uk 3,712,013.70 (6.882 %) / es 2,170,562.74 (5.261 %)**, `retired` **uk 10,964,343.30 (20.327 %) / es 9,308,468.47 (22.564 %)**, both partitions residual **0.00**. 🔴 **`retired` roughly DOUBLES and becomes the second-largest band (uk 14.02 → 20.33 %, es 14.99 → 22.56 %) — any statement about retired-person occupancy must name its base, and THE TWO FILES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE: `marginals_<c>.csv` is published fields on published bases, `econ_11plus_<c>.csv` is a CONVENTION applied on top. Step 5.2 rakes on the latter; anything citing a census figure quotes the former.** ⚪ `75+` = `retired` is NOT clean in Spain — `uk` 95.4 %, `it` 71.9 %, **`es` only 58.9 %** (251 `homemaker` + 539 `other_inactive` at 75+); quote it wherever the marginal is used. ⚪ `unknown` now carries real mass where `marginals_<c>.csv` has it as a `0` row flagged `NOT_PUBLISHED`; donors can carry it — declared crosswalk value in all three countries, and in Italy it IS the `11-14` band (`FINDING 48`). 🟢 **AT THE AUTHOR'S INSTRUCTION, TWICE: `4J_docs_occ/Resources/preprocessing_precedents.md` IS WRITTEN — an index of TEN 2J/3J preprocessing sources with the decisions extracted, to be consulted BEFORE any future preprocessing decision.** Standing rule saved to memory as `feedback_check_resources_before_preprocessing`. Its four reusable METHODS: (§2) how a "missing" column is investigated before being declared absent — the GSS 2005 `TELEWORK` case, where `MAR_Q190` was in the data the whole time while the pipeline had it all-NaN "by design", the same class of 