**would have PASSED it**. `V6.c` is shown load-bearing rather than stated, and it cost no GPU.

**Three ways the null could be built favourably, each a hard failure in code:** raking onto different
marginals than the model got (`score_margin` refuses to compare mismatched provenance labels, and a
blank label is refused too); leaving the held-out country in the donor pool (a self-donor makes the
null unbeatable *for the right reason* and nothing in the number would look wrong); a non-strict
comparison. **Plus two silent-failure guards:** a target category no donor can supply is a hard FAIL,
never a silent drop — 🔴 **watch this one when the marginals land, because the UK's
`strat_hh_type = unknown` (D-S3-14) is exactly the category a published census table will not have** —
and non-convergence raises instead of returning the last iterate, demonstrated on a structurally
infeasible pool that stalls at **22 pp** against a 0.5 pp tolerance.

**NOT closed:** the null has never been run on real data and cannot be until 5.1 exists; 23 unit tests
on synthetic pools are not a null. `score_margin()` does not choose the metric — that is still open,
and `FINDING 39`/`D-S6-3` bear on it. `V6.b` (scorer and gate read the same table) needs both to
exist; only one does. `G6.2` and `G6.3`'s nulls are still not built.

